# Writing VBA tests

The canonical guide for *authoring* a test in the VCS add-in or any Access
database using the VCS test runner. For the three testing layers, the round-trip
fixture harness, and where results land, see
[docs/testing-strategy.md](../../docs/testing-strategy.md); for `VCS.RunTests`
filter syntax, [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md); for the runner's web UI and bridge,
[docs/web-test-runner.md](../../docs/web-test-runner.md).

Tests assert with `TestAssert condition, "optional context"`, a drop-in
replacement for `Debug.Assert`. The context string identifies which assertion
failed. Under the test runner `TestAssert` reports back to the add-in; run
standalone (F5 or Immediate Window) it falls back to `Debug.Assert`.

## Standard module tests

Parameterless `Public Sub` procedures in a module identified as a test module:

```vba
Attribute VB_Name = "modTestMyFeature"
Option Compare Database
Option Explicit
Option Private Module
'@Folder("Tests")

Public Sub TestSomeBehavior()
    TestAssert MyFunction(42) = 84, "MyFunction should double input"
End Sub
```

## Class module tests (preferred for new tests)

Class modules get setup/teardown for free, and each test method runs against a
**fresh instance**, giving complete isolation:

```vba
Private Sub Class_Initialize()
    Set m_db = CurrentDb          ' before each test method
End Sub

Private Sub Class_Terminate()
    Set m_db = Nothing            ' after each test method
End Sub
```

Keep the standard `VERSION 1.0 CLASS` / `BEGIN` / `END` block, the
`Attribute VB_Name` line, and the `Option` / `'@Folder("Tests")` header, as in
any exported class file.

## Discovery rules

A module is a **test module** if it is a standard module (`vbext_ct_StdModule`) or
standalone class module (`vbext_ct_ClassModule`), AND either carries a
`'@Folder("...Tests...")` annotation (when the project uses `@Folder` anywhere) or
has `Test` in its name (case-insensitive fallback). Within one, a procedure is a
**test** if it is a `Public Sub` with zero parameters — or, in class modules, a
`Public Sub` or `Public Function` with zero parameters, excluding
`Class_Initialize` and `Class_Terminate`. To keep a helper out of discovery, make
it `Private` or give it a parameter (even an unused `Optional` one).

## Tagging

`'@Tag("name")` annotations categorize tests for filtering. Tags are
case-insensitive and appear in the JSON results under a `"tags"` array.
Module-level tags sit in the first ~30 lines, before any procedure, and apply to
every test in the module. Procedure-level tags go at the very top of the body,
before any `Dim`. The two sets are merged.

## Class test factory

The runner auto-maintains a `TestClassFactory` function inside `modTestAssert`,
one `Select Case` entry per test class. Do not hand-edit between the markers.

## Global suite hooks

Optional **once-per-run** setup/teardown in the target project's `modTestAssert`:
`GlobalTestSetup` runs before the first test, `GlobalTestTeardown` after all tests
and the results JSON. They ship as empty stubs in fresh installs, are skipped when
absent, and do not run when a filter matches nothing. Hook errors are non-fatal
and teardown still runs. `Class_Initialize` / `Class_Terminate` nest inside.

## Conventions

- Name test modules with `Test` in the name (`modTestEncoding`, `clsTestQueryParser`).
- Use `'@Folder("Tests")` for explicit discovery when the project has annotations.
- Use `Option Private Module` on standard test modules to keep test subs out of
  the macro list — the runner can still call them.
- One assertion concept per test method; use `context` to distinguish assertions.
- Do not add per-test `Operation.InteractionMode = eimSilent` guards; the runner
  owns root interaction policy (see below).

## Which project a test runs in

The *installed* add-in drives the run and owns the operation; test procedures
execute in the project being tested. For this repo those are two loaded copies of
the same project, so `Operation` inside a test is a different, idle instance from
the one hosting the run — its `Status`, `InteractionMode`, and `Attended` say
nothing about the run. What every hosted project can see is
`modTestAssert.TestRunActive`, true for the duration of the run.

## Three ways a test can sabotage its own run

- **Never call `Operation.TryBeginRoot`** (or legacy `Begin`/`Finish`) on the
  session operation. Where a root is active it is refused — a dialog on an
  attended run — or you complete the run's root, which runs `ReleaseObjects` and
  destroys `Log` and `TestRunner` mid-run. To test root lifecycle behavior, drive
  a private `New clsOperation`: no registry writes, no MCP callback, no singleton
  teardown. See `modTestOperationLifecycle`.
- **Never call `Log.Error`** on the session log. The runner reports any error
  logged during a test as a failure of that test, so such a test can never pass.
  Assert on the decision instead of the side effect.
- **Never open a prompt.** While a run is active `PromptWouldDisplay` is false for
  every prompt, gesture prompts included. Assert that rather than calling one, and
  use `MsgBox2` only where suppression is already established.
